# =============================================================================
# libera — capture-free modifier on the legacy lambda head
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • Legacy composition — `free` sits after the legacy parameter list and
#     before the colon, exactly as it sits before the `∴` in the compact
#     form: `lambda int x free: x * 2`
#   • Same contract — the marked closure may not reference any enclosing
#     local or parameter (CL-5 reject surface, see libera-reject.fab)
#   • The la reader spelling — `clausura numerus x libera: x * 2` is the la
#     twin of the same head
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • placing `free` after the colon — the modifier belongs to the
#     signature, not the body
#   • capturing an enclosing binding in a free-marked legacy closure — the
#     reject rule is identical to the compact form
#
# See also: libera, clausura, ∴
# =============================================================================

# libera — legacy lambda head with the free modifier
#
# GRAMMAR:
#   closureExpr :← 'lambda' params 'free' ':' expr
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   legacy-libera.expected — the doubled and summed values.

main {
    const (int) → int f ← int x ∴ x * 2
    print f(5)
    const (int, int) → int g ← (int x, int y) ∴ x + y
    print g(2, 3)
}
